# Idea Brief: Adaptive Pre-PRM Idea Readiness Review

Status: completed
Type: idea-brief
Updated: 2026-08-29
Next Action: none
Produces: MAP-0012

## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Formalize an adaptive readiness review between Tool Shed brainstorming and PRM. Operators currently
improve an Idea Brief by asking useful but easy-to-forget questions such as “Is this KISS?”, “What
are we missing?”, and “Is it ready to promote?” The quality of the handoff should not depend on the
operator remembering the same questions for every idea.

Add an explicit read-only route such as `ts: bs review <idea-id-or-path>`. It examines the current
Idea Brief and produces one compact verdict:

- `READY`: no material pre-promotion issue remains;
- `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES`: unresolved items are valid roadmap decisions, experiments, or evidence
  gates and do not require more brainstorming; or
- `NOT-READY`: a material ambiguity, contradiction, missing authority, or owner decision would make
  PRM guess or plan the wrong outcome.

Readiness review has exactly two execution entry points: an explicit operator request for the
manual review route, and `ts: prm idea <idea-id-or-path>`. Ordinary `ts: bs` turns never run the
review implicitly. Creating, editing, showing, listing, or resuming an Idea Brief may make a prior
review stale through a cheap revision comparison, but none of those actions performs semantic
review or adds review latency to the next brainstorming response.

`ts: prm idea <idea-id-or-path>` must enforce the same review automatically. It may reuse only a
current ready review—`READY` or `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES`—bound to the exact Idea Brief identity,
document revision/body hash, and readiness contract version. If no such current ready review
exists, including when the latest result is `NOT-READY`, PRM runs a new review before creating or
accepting project-map direction.

When the result is `READY` or `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES`, PRM continues without turning the review into
an approval ceremony. It carries named PRM gates into the proposed plan and opens the governed
outcome loop from the reviewed idea revision. When the result is `NOT-READY`, promotion stops and
Tool Shed engages the operator on the smallest material blocker, with context, consequences,
credible choices, and a recommendation. An operator response may settle the issue, defer scope, or
reclassify it as a PRM gate; the brief is updated and the now-stale review is rerun. The system must
not bypass a real blocker through a generic override or fabricate the missing decision.

### Why It Matters

Brainstorming already preserves possibilities, constraints, open questions, decisions, and a
current synthesis, but it has no consistent final review. Good results currently depend on
operator persistence and the particular questions raised in conversation. A lightweight,
revision-bound review makes promotion quality repeatable while preserving free-form discovery.

This also prevents two opposite failures: promoting an underspecified idea that forces PRM to
invent intent, and keeping an adequately understood idea in brainstorming because implementation
details have not yet been decided. Explicit `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES` classification lets PRM own the
right uncertainties without hiding actual product or authority decisions.

### Desired Outcome

An operator can brainstorm naturally and promote an idea without remembering a manual closing
question sequence. Tool Shed automatically verifies that the accepted outcome is clear enough for
PRM, exposes only material blockers, and carries legitimate design and evidence questions forward
as named gates. Every result is traceable to the exact reviewed Idea Brief revision, becomes stale
when its inputs change, and supports the idea’s later origin-to-product reconciliation. Normal
brainstorming remains as responsive as it is without this feature because review runs only when
explicitly requested or when promotion is invoked.

## Trigger Boundary

Only these actions execute readiness review:

1. The operator explicitly invokes the manual route, such as
   `ts: bs review <idea-id-or-path>`.
2. The operator invokes `ts: prm idea <idea-id-or-path>` and no fresh ready review is reusable.

No other route runs it. In particular, ordinary brainstorming, Idea Brief creation or update,
status/list/show/overview, background maintenance, indexing, rendering generated views, database
checkpointing, and startup do not perform readiness review. They may report that an existing
review is current, stale, or absent using exact metadata comparisons only.

If a manual review returns `NOT-READY`, later ordinary brainstorming may resolve its findings and
make that result stale, but it does not automatically rerun. A new result appears only after
another explicit manual review or a later PRM promotion attempt. If `NOT-READY` occurs inside an
active PRM-triggered operator dialogue, Tool Shed may rerun after the operator’s answer because the
original PRM invocation remains the active review trigger. Choosing to return to ordinary
brainstorming ends that triggered flow.

## Review Contract

### Minimum Universal Review

Every review checks only the smallest promotion-critical set:

- the desired outcome and why it matters are understandable;
- the first useful boundary and explicit non-goals are sufficient to prevent obvious scope drift;
- settled decisions, credible alternatives, and remaining questions do not contradict each other;
- each unresolved item is classified as a pre-promotion blocker, PRM gate, or deferred possibility;
- KISS concerns identify unjustified current complexity without demanding a mandatory KISS field;
- target, ownership, authority, and current/product-truth boundaries are clear enough for planning;
  and
- the reviewed origin, accepted requirements, future material-change handling, evidence endpoint,
  verdict, residuals, and upward reconciliation can enter a governed closed loop.

The review reports only material findings. Absence of optional template sections, prose style, or
implementation detail is not itself a failure.

### Adaptive Risk Modules

Additional checks activate only from evidence in the brief:

- authentication, secrets, privacy, enrollment, or external data;
- database authority, conversion, migration, backup, recovery, or data-loss risk;
- background workers, scheduling, retries, concurrency, or liveness;
- deployment, production access, release, rollback, or live-service compatibility;
- cross-platform, disconnected installation, upgrade, or backward compatibility;
- external services, repositories, accounts, purchases, or protected environments; and
- preservation of an existing product, API, data contract, or visual presentation.

The review explains why each adaptive module activated. It does not run a universal heavyweight
checklist against simple ideas.

### Result Shape

A compact human and JSON result should include:

- idea artifact ID, visible ID, document revision, body hash, and review-contract version;
- verdict, review time, reviewer/actor, and a result digest;
- promotion blockers with the exact ambiguity and decision owner;
- PRM gates with the milestone or evidence concern they must constrain;
- deferred items that cannot silently enter first-release scope;
- contradictions, duplication, or unjustified complexity worth fixing; and
- concise recommended brief updates or operator questions.

The result is a review record, not a second editable Idea Brief. The brief remains the narrative
authority. Tool Shed should reuse existing database operation/event/document primitives before
adding schema; if a dedicated readiness record is necessary, it must have one structured authority
and a generated read-only view rather than a competing Markdown file.

## PRM Enforcement Flow

1. Resolve the exact Idea Brief and current database/document revision.
2. Find the latest ready readiness review for the same artifact ID.
3. Treat it as current only when its verdict is `READY` or `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES` and its idea
   revision, body hash, and review-contract version all match.
4. If no current ready review exists—including an absent, stale, or latest `NOT-READY` result—run
   the adaptive review synchronously and persist the structured result.
5. On `READY`, continue normal PRM behavior.
6. On `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES`, continue and carry every named gate into the proposed project map or
   roadmap without silently resolving it.
7. On `NOT-READY`, do not create or accept project-map direction. Engage the operator on one or a
   small related set of material blockers, starting with the highest-leverage issue.
8. Apply only the operator’s explicit decisions to the Idea Brief, rerun because the prior review
   is stale while the original PRM-triggered dialogue remains active, and continue automatically
   once the result becomes ready. If the operator returns to ordinary brainstorming, stop the
   review flow and wait for one of the two defined triggers.

Operator engagement is not a generic “approve?” prompt. It states what is missing, why PRM cannot
safely infer it, the effect of each plausible choice, the recommended choice, and whether the issue
can instead be deferred or carried as a gate. The operator can also stop, park the idea, or continue
brainstorming.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Keep brainstorming conversational and free-form; do not require the operator to fill a form.
- Never run readiness review during an ordinary brainstorming turn unless that turn explicitly
  invokes the manual review route.
- Do not trigger review from an Idea Brief write, stale transition, list/show/status/overview,
  generated-view render, checkpoint, startup, background task, or elapsed time.
- Do not add a required KISS section or treat KISS as an independent approval gate.
- Do not require every implementation decision before PRM; carry legitimate uncertainty as gates.
- Do not reuse a review after any Idea Brief content revision or readiness-contract change.
- Do not let a semantic review claim deterministic certainty. Scripts should validate identity,
  freshness, result structure, and routing; the agent supplies evidence-backed semantic judgment.
- Do not invent owner intent, authority, historical facts, evidence, or a default that materially
  changes the outcome.
- Do not create a second editable Markdown review artifact for every run.
- Do not interrupt on warnings, style preferences, or questions that PRM can safely own.
- Preserve old reviews as history when useful, but expose only the current result in normal status.
- Support existing Idea Briefs incrementally: their first later promotion attempt runs the review;
  no bulk rewrite or backfill is required.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Operator manually asks closing questions | No product change | Inconsistent and easy to forget | Current behavior; insufficient |
| Always run one fixed exhaustive checklist | Predictable coverage | Ceremony, irrelevant questions, and checklist gaming | Rejected |
| Adaptive semantic review plus deterministic freshness enforcement | Covers material risk while remaining proportional | Requires a clear contract between agent judgment and script enforcement | Selected direction |
| Review only when explicitly requested | Useful diagnostic without changing PRM | Promotion can still bypass review | Insufficient alone |
| Automatically review at PRM entry | No forgotten final check; one consistent transition | Must avoid stale reuse and unnecessary interruption | Required |
| Review after every brainstorming update | Findings always current | Adds latency to the operator’s iterative discovery loop | Rejected |
| Treat every open question as a blocker | Simple rule | Prevents PRM from owning design and evidence gates | Rejected |
| Let an operator override `NOT-READY` directly | Fast escape hatch | Can erase the very material ambiguity the review exists to expose | Rejected; operator may resolve, defer, or reclassify the issue explicitly |
| Store each review as Markdown | Easy to browse | Adds file noise and competing editable state | Not selected |
| Store a compact structured result with generated view | Freshness and queryability without collateral noise | May require a bounded data-model addition | Preferred, reuse existing primitives first |

## Open Questions

- Should the explicit route be `ts: bs review <idea>`, `ts: review idea <idea>`, or support both as
  aliases around one contract?
- Which existing hybrid database primitives can store the structured result without schema growth?
- Which changes beyond the Idea Brief body—such as linked target or authority changes—must also
  invalidate a review?
- Should `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES` require a generated gate-transfer manifest so PRM can prove that no
  named gate was dropped?
- How should an interrupted operator dialogue resume without asking already settled questions?
- What compact review-contract versioning and upgrade behavior prevents older clients from treating
  an unknown verdict or check as current?
- Which representative simple, moderate, and consequential Idea Brief fixtures should prove that
  the adaptive review neither misses material blockers nor adds ceremony to small ideas?

## Decisions

- Add an explicit adaptive, read-only Idea Brief readiness review.
- Permit exactly two review triggers: an explicit manual review request and a PRM idea promotion
  attempt that lacks a fresh ready review.
- Keep ordinary brainstorming review-free; Idea Brief changes invalidate review freshness without
  executing another review.
- Use `READY`, `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES`, and `NOT-READY` as the three operator-facing outcomes.
- Make `ts: prm idea` automatically run the review when no current ready result exists, including
  when the latest result is `NOT-READY`.
- Bind review freshness to the exact idea artifact ID, document revision/body hash, and readiness
  contract version; changed input makes the review stale.
- Continue automatically for both ready outcomes, carrying named PRM gates forward.
- When not ready, stop promotion and engage the operator on material blockers rather than asking a
  generic approval question.
- During an active review-triggered operator dialogue, rerun after the operator’s Idea Brief change
  and continue when ready. Outside that active trigger, mark the prior result stale and wait for a
  new manual review request or PRM promotion attempt.
- Keep structural/freshness enforcement deterministic and semantic readiness evidence-backed.
- Apply adaptive risk checks only when the idea activates them.
- Avoid a new editable Markdown artifact per review; prefer structured database state plus a
  generated view if existing primitives are insufficient.

## Don't Forget

- “What are we missing?” is the core coverage question; it should not mean “add every conceivable
  requirement.”
- A current review is about a specific Idea Brief revision, not merely the same title or path.
- `READY-WITH-PRM-GATES` is necessary to prevent implementation questions from bloating discovery.
- A deferred item must remain visible so PRM does not silently pull it into first-release scope.
- Operator engagement should minimize round trips by grouping only tightly related blockers and
  offering a recommendation.
- Promotion review is a quality boundary, not a new lifecycle approval ceremony.
- Stale detection should be a cheap metadata comparison; semantic review must never become a
  background or per-turn tax.
- The readiness review must itself be covered by normal Tool Shed upgrade, compatibility,
  validation, and closed-loop outcome reconciliation when implemented.

